        "Ask your grandparents what the night sky looked like when they were children, and many of them will describe something most young people have never seen: a pale band of light stretching from one side of the sky to the other. That band is the Milky Way, the view we get of our own galaxy from inside it. Today, according to one international study, more than a third of the world’s population — and the great majority of people in Japan, Europe and North America — can no longer see it from where they live. {u:(1):The stars have not gone anywhere. What has changed is the sky between us and them.}",
