        "Many living things depend on the regular change between day and night. Newly hatched sea turtles find their way to the ocean by moving toward the brightest horizon, which on a natural beach is the sky over the water. When a hotel or a road behind the beach is brighter, the young turtles crawl inland instead, where many die. Birds that migrate at night use the stars to navigate, and lit-up buildings can confuse them so badly that they circle until they are exhausted or fly into the glass. Even insects suffer: a single street lamp on a summer night can attract and kill thousands of moths, {b:イ} in turn reduces the food available for bats and the pollination of night-blooming plants.",
